  "worked_solution": "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ terms and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}+16x-20 & =0 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}+16x)-9{y}^{2}-20 & =0 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}+4x]-9{y}^{2}-20 & =0 \\\\ 4[(x+2)^{2}-4]-9{y}^{2}-20 & =0 \\\\ 4(x+2)^{2}-16-9{y}^{2}-20 & =0 \\\\ 4(x+2)^{2}-9{y}^{2} & =20+16 \\\\ 4(x+2)^{2}-9{y}^{2} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{4(x+2)^{2}}{36}-\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{36} & =\\frac{36}{36} \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nh=-2,\\,k=0,\\,a=\\sqrt{9}=3,\\,b=\\sqrt{4}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
